Goose has no way for a program to walk the fields of a struct. So code that does the same thing for each field has to be written by hand, once per field: saving and loading, debug printing, comparison, resetting to defaults, and so on. In my project, a Python script that reads the source with regular expressions and generates the save code fills this gap.

Proposed form:

```goose
fields(v) { name, f => ... }    // each field's name and value, in declaration order
fields(&v) { name, f => ... }   // each field's name and a reference to the field
```

Its meaning is defined only as "the compiler rewrites it into calls of the function value, one per field":

```goose
fields(v) F   ->  F("turn", v.turn); F("hp", v.hp); F("told", v.told);
fields(&v) F  ->  F("turn", &v.turn); F("hp", &v.hp); F("told", &v.told);
```

- What it rewrites to is ordinary code you can write in Goose today. Type checking, reference lifetimes and code generation need no new mechanism.
- The function value is checked for each field at that field's type (§7.7). Nested structs and arrays can be walked with an overload per type.
- No type information is kept at run time; everything is expanded at compile time.

I wrote the rewritten form by hand and confirmed that it runs on `v2026.1` (example below).

```goose
struct Told { notice: i64, a: i64 }
struct W { turn: i64, asked: bool, hp: i64[4], told: Told }

// what fields would rewrite to, written by hand
fn each_field<F>(v: W) { F("turn", v.turn); F("asked", v.asked); F("hp", v.hp); F("told", v.told); }
fn each_field<F>(v: Told) { F("notice", v.notice); F("a", v.a); }
fn each_field_ref<F>(v: W&) { F("turn", &v.turn); F("asked", &v.asked); F("hp", &v.hp); F("told", &v.told); }
fn each_field_ref<F>(v: Told&) { F("notice", &v.notice); F("a", &v.a); }

fn put(path: u8[>..], x: i64) { print(path, " = ", x); }
fn put(path: u8[>..], x: bool) { print(path, " = ", if x { 1 } else { 0 }); }
fn put(path: u8[>..], xs: i64[4]) { for x, i in xs { print(path, "[", i, "] = ", x); } }
fn put(path: u8[>..], s) { each_field(s) { name, f => put(str(path, ".", name), f) } }

fn bump(x: i64&) { x = x + 1; }
fn bump(x: bool&) { x = !x; }
fn bump(xs: i64[4]&) { for &x in xs { x = x + 1; } }
fn bump(s) { each_field_ref(s) { name, f => bump(f) } }

fn main() {
    var w = W { turn: 7, asked: false, hp: [1, 2, 3, 4], told: Told { notice: 5, a: 9 } };
    bump(&w);
    put(str("W"), w);
}
```

