## Problem

A function that nothing calls, whose calls reach a `return ... from` it does not enclose, is rejected, though the program is valid:

```goose
fn emit(n: i64) {
    if n < 0 { return false from guarded; }
}

fn guarded(n: i64) -> bool {
    emit(n);
    return true;
}

fn emit_twice(n: i64) {
    emit(n);
    emit(n + 1);
}

fn main() {
    print(guarded(1), " ", guarded(-1));
}
```

On origin/master (ac9de76a), Debug build with `-DGOOSE_BUILTIN_LIBS=all`:

```
$ build/goose unreached.goose
parsed ok: 4 top-level declarations, 1 file(s)
unreached.goose:11: error: call to emit requires an enclosing call of guarded (it does `return ... from guarded`)
    emit(n);
  in fn emit_twice(i64) instantiated from unreached.goose:10
```

With this change:

```
$ build/goose unreached.goose
parsed ok: 4 top-level declarations, 1 file(s)
typechecked ok: 3 specialization(s), 0 struct/0 enum instance(s); optimized -O1: 3 inlined, 0 base case(s), 5 folded, 4 propagated, 0 tail loop(s), 0 reassociated
bce: elided 0/0 index and 0/0 slice checks
true false
```

Without `emit_twice` the same program compiles and prints `true false` on origin/master.

## Why it is valid

§7.9 requires that "every call site of the returning function must lie within the dynamic extent of an `f` call", checked "in call-graph order": on every call path from `main` to the `return ... from`, a call of `guarded` must come first. `emit_twice` is on no call path, so it has no call that violates this, and a call of it under `guarded` would be valid. The checker already accepts this when the `return ... from` is written in the uncalled function's own body: `CheckUnreached` skips such a function (`ScanForeignFrom`), since checked on its own it could only be rejected. The same holds one call further away, which is how a writer helper looks that returns from the call its own callers run it under.

## Cause

After main and the thread entry points, `TypeCheck::CheckUnreached` checks each remaining non-generic top-level function standalone (`CheckStandalone`), with no caller. It skips one whose body has a `return ... from` an outside target, but only by scanning that body. A call in `emit_twice` to `emit` makes the specialization of `emit` that does `return ... from guarded` with no enclosing `guarded`, which is an error for the call path that the standalone check invents.

## Fix

`CheckUnreached` also skips a function from which a call path reaches a `return ... from` with no function of the target's name on the path (`ReachesForeignFrom`). The first call computes, for every top-level function, the leaf names of the targets it reaches: the `return ... from`s in its body and in its nested functions, minus the names it encloses (its own and its nested functions'), then the targets of its callees to a fixed point, never passing a function of the target's name. Calls are followed by leaf name before resolution: every `Ident` or `Dot` name in a body counts as a call of every top-level function of that name, in any namespace. That finds more paths than the real ones, so more functions are skipped, never fewer.

This only affects functions no call reaches. A function that is called is checked through its calls as before, and a `return ... from` with no enclosing call on a real path is still an error. A skipped function's other errors are not reported until something calls it, as is already the case for one with the `return ... from` in its own body. `docs/implementation.md` §3.1, which lists what the unreached check covers, says so.

## Testing

- New `test/typing/unreached_return_from_callee.goose` (expected output `true false`): uncalled functions that reach `emit`'s `return false from guarded` through a direct call, through a nested function calling another, and by passing `emit` as a function value to a generic `apply<F>`. On origin/master it fails with `call to emit requires an enclosing call of guarded` at line 15 (in `emit_twice`) and line 24 (in `apply<F = emit>`, from `emit_value`). With the fix it passes.
- New `test/errors_tc/return_from_missing_through_callee.goose`: the same `emit_twice`, called from `main` outside `guarded`, is still rejected ("call to emit requires an enclosing call of guarded"), before and after the fix. The existing return-from rejections (`from_missing`, `ns_from_other`, `ns_from_unknown`, `return_from_*`, `generic_return_from_range`, `from_holder_escape`) pass unchanged.
- Full suite on macOS (arm64), built as CI builds it (`-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DGOOSE_BUILTIN_LIBS=all`), `python3 test/run_tests.py --exe build/goose --profile baseline --cc native --require-clang`: C backend clang 21.0.0, JIT backend TinyCC, all tests passed (parse/roundtrip/typecheck 1155 ok, generated C 577 ok, JIT 670 ok, errors_tc 1854 ok, samples 61 ok).

