Goose packs values with no padding, so a scalar on a data stack or in a packed type can start at any address. Generated C reads and writes those values through plain `int64_t *`, `uint32_t *`, `double *` and pointer casts. That is undefined behaviour in C, even on hardware that allows the access, and UBSan's alignment check reports every one. The sanitize profile hides this with `-fno-sanitize=alignment`.

This matters when Goose output is embedded somewhere that runs UBSan on it. CRAN, R's package repository, checks every package's compiled code with gcc and clang `-fsanitize=undefined`, alignment included. An R package that ships Goose-generated C ([zugojson](https://github.com/pedrobtz/zugojson)) failed those checks at 14 sites, all in generated code.

The fix is to say in C what the code already does. `runtime.h` gets `gs_ua_i16` through `gs_ua_f64`, typedefs with `__attribute__((aligned(1)))`. Codegen uses them wherever it forms a pointer into packed memory:
- casts;
- typed pointer locals;
- the element pointer of a scalar slice;
- a reference to a scalar.

A pointer stored in packed memory gets an unaligned typedef of its own, emitted on first use. Packed structs and one-byte types need nothing. On compilers without the attribute (MSVC, TinyCC) the typedefs are the plain types, so nothing changes there.

On x86-64 and arm64 the instructions are the same: an unaligned `mov` either way. In an interleaved A/B benchmark of zugojson, three runs each, every case was within ±3% with no consistent direction, and the `.so` text size is the same.

- `src/runtime/runtime.h`: `GS_UNALIGNED` and the `gs_ua_*` typedefs, under a `GS_UA_TYPES` guard.
- `src/codegen_types.h`, `src/codegen.h`: `UCT`, `UIntCT` and `URelCT`, the unaligned counterparts of `CT`, `IntCT` and `RelCT`. Scalar slice element pointers use `UCT`.
- `src/codegen_*.h`: every site that casts packed memory to a pointer, or declares a pointer into it, uses the unaligned type. That is 106 lines, almost all a one-word change.
- `src/codegen_emit.h`: an exported header (`--header`) carries the same typedefs, because a scalar slice in an export's signature now names them.
- `scripts/toolchain.py`: the sanitize profile drops `-fno-sanitize=alignment`, so the suite checks this from now on.
- `docs/testing.md`, `docs/implementation.md` §6.1: what changed and why.
- `src/runtime_inline.h`: regenerated.

Tested:
- the baseline suite on macOS (x86-64): all tests pass;
- the suite with `-fsanitize=alignment`, which the sanitize profile only runs on Linux, so this was a local run on macOS: 454 failing runs before the change and none after, apart from the samples runner, which refuses the profile on macOS;
- the Linux sanitize job here has not run on it yet;
- an export taking `i64[:]`, called from C with a slice that starts one byte off, under UBSan;
- the same change cherry-picked onto `e76d5c9`, as zugojson pins it. On Linux, its CI is green with CRAN's gcc and clang UBSan flags, and in the R-hub `clang-asan` and `gcc-asan` containers, where the unpatched code fails ([zugojson#8](https://github.com/pedrobtz/zugojson/pull/8), against [#7](https://github.com/pedrobtz/zugojson/pull/7)).

