## What happens

`binary` reads `u16`, `i16` and `u32` fields in little-endian order, at offsets and on a `Reader`. That fits WAD files, which it was added for in 03ae1626. PNG, JPEG, Java class files, Mach-O universal headers and most network protocols store their fields big-endian, so a program reading one takes the bytes with `read_bytes` and joins them itself. Tested with the v2026.3 release; `stdlib/binary.goose` is unchanged on master cce94b91.

## Reproduce

A PNG chunk length is a big-endian `u32`:

```goose
