## What the docs say

docs/stdlib.md describes `binary` as "Checked, little-endian reads of external formats, written entirely in Goose." So this is a question of scope: would you take big-endian reads in `binary`, or do you see them as a package's job?

## Options

1. Add `read_u16be`, `read_i16be` and `read_u32be`: the widths `binary` already has, in its existing shape (the value and a `bool`, zero and `false` on a bad range, `pos` moved only on success). That is six functions counting the `Reader` forms, 28 lines in `stdlib/binary.goose`. I have it on a branch with tests in `test/stdlib/stdlib_binary.goose` (the PNG signature and a chunk length, signed and unaligned values, hostile offsets, the cursor after a failed read), the lists in docs/stdlib.md, and the one-line mention in the C++ and Rust guide. `python3 test/run_tests.py --profile baseline --cc native --require-clang` reports "all tests passed" on the branch, built on cce94b91 (macOS 26.7.1, M1 Max, Apple clang 17.0.0); Linux and Windows were not run. Neither byte order has a 64-bit read yet; that would be a separate step.
2. One read over the type and the byte order, as Zig's `std.mem.readInt(u32, bytes, .big)` does, in place of separate names. Go's `encoding/binary` has both: `binary.BigEndian.Uint32(b)` names the width, and `BigEndian` is a value of the `ByteOrder` interface, which `binary.Read` takes as a parameter. Rust's integers have the named form, `u32::from_be_bytes`.
3. Leave it to packages. A package can do this today on top of `binary::read_bytes`, which already checks the range: the loop above, once per width. In Rust, many programs use the `byteorder` crate for this.

Option 1 is plain Goose that returns values, so it allocates nothing, copies nothing and shares nothing between threads. It keeps to the README's "No allocator, no GC, no reference counting, no destructors" and "Memory safe with zero annotations", and touches no other point of "Why Goose".

The example code [here](https://aardappel.github.io/goose/docs/stdlib.html#ui) shows that `ui::slider_float(0.0, volume, 1.0, 0.05);` being used to modify the volume variable, but this doesn't work. The actual code is `volume = ...`. `slider_float` doesn't take the float value by reference, so is unable to modify it.

However, the other ui function use in that example `ui::checkbox_label` does take the bool by reference.

```rust
volume = ctx.slide_float(0.0, volume, 1.0, 0.05);
ctx.checkbox_label("Muted", muted);
```

Hi Wouter,

While marking functions `checkfree` (§7.14) in a larger program, I ran into five places where an index or an index-type conversion keeps its check although it cannot fail. Two are on §7.14's list of what an implementation must prove, and three get a different verdict at different `-O` levels. The check stays in the generated code of ordinary functions too; `checkfree` only made it visible. This PR fixes the five, one commit each, plus a soundness bug found along the way.

| # | Case | Before: -O0 | Before: -O1/-O2 | Required by §7.14 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | a call result of `a`'s index type used directly as an index into `a` | proved | **kept** | yes |
| 2 | a call writing an array of structs drops a length fact about another array | **kept** | proved | yes |
| 3 | a function's own facts are lost once it is inlined into a loop | proved | **kept** | no |
| 4 | `if`/`else` joins drop range facts | kept | kept | no |
| 5 | `u32` arithmetic is not related to the bound | kept | kept | no |

### 1. A call result of `a`'s index type, used directly as an index into `a`

```goose
var nodes: f64[>..];
type Id = u32 in nodes;
type Link = u32 upto nodes;
checkfree fn child(l: Link) -> Id { let k: i64 = l; assert(k > 0); return (k - 1) as Id; }
checkfree fn direct(l: Link) -> f64 { return nodes[child(l)]; }   // -O1/-O2: "the length of nodes is not one the analysis tracks here"
```

`Index::BceWalk` dropped the receiver's length when walking the index bumped *any* generation. Once `child` is inlined, its own `let`s do, so `nodes.len` was thrown away although nothing touched it. Now it is dropped only when the receiver's own place changed, which every rebind, growth, shrink or overwrite of it bumps. A second gap showed up in testing: an inlined body with several returns has no value term, and `TermOf` returned before the fallback every index-typed value gets. It now falls back to `IndexTypeTerm`, as the call's result does at -O0.

### 2. A call writing an array of structs drops a fact about another array

```goose
struct Tax { rate: i64, amount: f64 }
root struct State { items: f64[>..] }
root struct Books { taxes: Tax[..101], lines: f64[>..] }
checkfree fn add_tax(taxes: Tax[..101]&, rate: i64, amount: f64) { … taxes.push(…); }
checkfree fn f(s: State&, b: Books&, k: i64) -> f64 {
    assert(k >= 0 && k < s.items.len);
    add_tax(b.taxes, 22, 1.0);
    return s.items[k];          // -O0: "nothing shows k < s.items.len"
}
```

`KillArgStorage` narrows a callee's write to arrays of the argument's own type only when the elements are scalars. For a `Tax[..101]` it applied no filter and killed every opaque place, `s.items` included. A direct `push` in `f` was already proved, which is why -O1/-O2 (the call inlined) accepted it. The narrowing now applies to any element type holding no length and no reference (`!HoldsLen(sub, true)`, the predicate `PointeeWriteKill` already uses): such an array holds no other array and leads to none.

### 3. A function's own facts are lost once it is inlined into a loop

```goose
checkfree fn probe(xs: f64[:], k: i64) -> f64 {
    assert(xs.len > 0);
    let m = xs.len - 1;
    var i = k & m;
    while xs[i] < 0.0 { i = (i + 1) & m; }    // -O1/-O2, inlined below: "nothing shows i < xs.len"
    return xs[i];
}
fn main() { … for j in 3 { s += probe(ys, j); } }
```

As a call, `probe` is proved at every level, even from callers that give it no length facts. Inlined into the loop, `xs` is declared anew each iteration, `RebindKill` bumps its place as a possible shrink, and `RunSpec` then refuses the `i <= len(xs) - 1` invariant. A rebind cannot just be ignored: an old `i` against a new `xs` would be wrong. So rebinds go to their own kill set instead of the shrink set, and the flow tracks which variables a write has placed below the length since the last rebind (`lestated`). `LeLenHolds` grants the invariant only to those, `RebindKill` removes them, and `Meet` intersects them. The recording walk is unchanged.

### 4. `if`/`else` joins drop range facts

```goose
checkfree fn clamp_let(xs: f64[:], k0: i64) -> f64 {
    guard xs.len > 0 else { return 0.0; }
    assert(k0 >= 0);
    let k = if k0 < xs.len { k0 } else { xs.len - 1 };
    return xs[k];                              // "nothing shows k >= 0"
}
checkfree fn clamp_var(xs: f64[:], k0: i64) -> f64 {
    guard xs.len > 0 else { return 0.0; }
    let last = xs.len - 1;
    var k = k0;
    assert(k >= 0);
    if k > last { k = last; }
    return xs[k];                              // "nothing shows k < xs.len"
}
```

`Meet` keeps only the facts both branches state literally. A variable assigned in one branch has a new generation there that the other branch never mentions, an `if` value has no term at all, and `k >= 0` on the `else` side holds only through `xs.len > 0`. These are plain difference constraints, not the disjunctions of TODO 0f. `Meet` now takes phis: one base per joined value (an `if` value, or an integer variable whose generation differs between the branches). For each branch it adds `p == that branch's term` to a scratch copy, runs `DistsFrom` from the phi both ways, and keeps the weaker of the two branches' implied bounds. It does nothing extra at joins without phis. Clamping an index is common enough that this seemed worth the cost. On a deliberately bad function (30 variables, 400 clamps), `-O1 --check` went from 0.04s to about 0.35s; on the samples plus `goose_in_goose` it was within noise.

### 5. `u32` arithmetic is not related to the bound

```goose
var nodes: f64[>..];
type Id = u32 in nodes;
type Link = u32 upto nodes;
checkfree fn link(e: Id) -> Link { return (e + 1) as Link; }   // "the analysis cannot relate (e + 1) to nodes.len"
```

`TermOf` gives no term to `x ± c` narrower than 64 bits. `NarrowStepTerm` now gives it a fresh base in the type's range, bounded on the side a wrap cannot cross: since `x` lies in the type and `|c| < 2^N`, a wrap moves the result by 2^N against the sign of `c`. So `s <= x + c` for `c >= 0`, `x + c <= s` for `c <= 0`, and both where the facts show it fits. For `e: u32 in nodes`, `e + 1 <= nodes.len` whether it wraps or not.

### Soundness bug: a block's value read past its `defer`

`BlockValueTerm` reads a block's value after `Block::BceWalk` has applied the kills of its `defer` bodies, so the term can name a variable the defer has since changed. On master this reads `xs[100]` of a 4-element array without a check (`--no-bce` aborts with "index 100 out of bounds", as it should):

```goose
fn deferred(xs: f64[:], z: i64) -> f64 {
    var x = 100;
    let k = { defer { x = z; } x };
    guard x >= 0 && x < xs.len else { return 0.0; }
    return xs[k];
}
fn main() {
    let xs: f64[>..] = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0];
    var zs: i64[>..] = [1];
    print(deferred(xs, zs[0]));    // prints 0.0 at -O0 and -O2
}
```

`BlockValueTerm` now gives no term for a block with a `defer`. It is in the case 4 commit, whose phis would have exposed it more, and `bce_join_values.goose` covers it.

### Testing

On master (2ed1a63), Release build, macOS arm64:

- `python3 test/run_tests.py --cc native`: all tests passed, with 68 bounds-check annotations (4 new fixtures plus an extended `bce_typed_kills.goose`), the goose_in_goose bootstrap and the samples. Each new fixture fails on master on exactly its new elisions and keeps.
- Differential fuzzing, each program at -O1/-O2 with the pass on and with `--no-bce`: `bench/bce_fuzz.py` (300 programs), plus two small fuzzers for the shapes these commits touch: rebinds around masked probes, and joins of clamped indices with off-by-ones. 0 mismatches.
- `--bce-lines` over the 2813 test and sample files, against master: at -O1 114 lines gain an elision (9947 → 10268 checks elided), at -O2 129 lines (11504 → 11889). No exit status changes. The only check kept that master elided is the `defer` case above.
- Not run: the sanitize profile, Linux and Windows, the native tests that need cmake, gfx/audio/ui.

`docs/implementation.md` is updated where the analysis is described (§5.2, §5.3, §5.5, §5.6, §5.7, §5.11).

Not covered: `match` arm values (only `if` values get a phi); a variable shifted in place by `+=` in one branch; and case 2 when the whole root is passed (`add_tax(b)` writing `b.taxes`), which needs a per-specialization "these two parameter classes are disjoint" relation. The simple version is unsound when a component and its owner are passed separately.

Cases 3 and 4 are the larger changes; if you don't like them, they need rethinking.

