### Problem Description

When multiple packages with native C shims reside within the same directory tree or share the same package root directory (`p.root`), `NativeBuildDir` in `src/native.h` hashes `p.root` (`Hex8(p.root)`) to the identical build directory, creating the identical response file path `nb.rsp` (e.g. `link-cc.rsp` or `link-msvc.rsp`).

In `src/main.cpp` (inside `Main()`, around lines 1820–1835):

```cpp
vector<string> rsps;
for (auto pkg : nativepkgs) {
    auto &p = astp->packages[pkg];
    if (p.builtin >= 0) {
        rsps.push_back(BuiltinPackageLinkFile(builtin_packages[p.builtin], tc.style, DirOf(exe)));
        continue;
    }
    NativeBuild nb;
    if (!BuildNativePart(*astp, pkg, filename, b, false, nb)) return 1;
    rsps.push_back(nb.rsp);
}
return BuildExecutable(filename, outfile, builddir, tc, buildopts, msgs, rsps, astp->packages[0].output);
```

Because `rsps.push_back(...)` does not check for existing entries, `rsps` receives duplicate entries for the same `.rsp` file path.

When `BuildExecutableCC` in `src/native.h` invokes GCC/Clang:
```cpp
for (auto &rsp : rsps) cmd += " @" + rsp;
```
The command line receives `@<path>/link-cc.rsp @<path>/link-cc.rsp`.

Because response files list compiled `.o` object files directly rather than static archives (`.a`), passing duplicate object files causes MinGW GCC (`ld.exe`) and GNU `ld` to fail with multiple definition errors:

```text
C:/msys64/ucrt64/bin/../lib/gcc/x86_64-w64-mingw32/14.2.0/../../../../x86_64-w64-mingw32/bin/ld.exe: C:/Users/.../.goose/build/pkg/d92f9a93/rio_probe.o: in function 'wing_rio_probe':
.../rio_probe.c:12: multiple definition of 'wing_rio_probe'; C:/Users/.../.goose/build/pkg/d92f9a93/rio_probe.o:.../rio_probe.c:12: first defined here
collect2.exe: error: ld returned 1 exit status
```

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Have a Goose project where two imported packages reside in the same repository root and declare native C shims in their native directory.
2. Compile with `goose build --cc gcc <file.goose>`.
3. Linker fails with `multiple definition of ...` due to `@link-cc.rsp` passed twice.

### Proposed Fix

Deduplicate entries before adding them to `rsps`:

```diff
--- a/src/main.cpp
+++ b/src/main.cpp
@@ -1821,13 +1821,16 @@ int Main(int argc, char **argv) {
                 for (auto pkg : nativepkgs) {
                     auto &p = astp->packages[pkg];
                     if (p.builtin >= 0) {
-                        rsps.push_back(BuiltinPackageLinkFile(builtin_packages[p.builtin],
-                                                              tc.style, DirOf(exe)));
+                        auto file = BuiltinPackageLinkFile(builtin_packages[p.builtin],
+                                                              tc.style, DirOf(exe));
+                        if (std::find(rsps.begin(), rsps.end(), file) == rsps.end())
+                            rsps.push_back(file);
                         continue;
                     }
                     NativeBuild nb;
                     if (!BuildNativePart(*astp, pkg, filename, b, false, nb)) return 1;
-                    rsps.push_back(nb.rsp);
+                    if (std::find(rsps.begin(), rsps.end(), nb.rsp) == rsps.end())
+                        rsps.push_back(nb.rsp);
                 }
                 return BuildExecutable(filename, outfile, builddir, tc, buildopts, msgs, rsps,
                                        astp->packages[0].output);
```

This fixes how a call replays its callee's stores (§5.1) when a holder goes through a helper by value. It covers two symptoms with one cause: a false rejection, and a use after shrink that is accepted when another caller of the same helper is checked first.

### Code wrongly rejected 

```goose
import std;

struct Row { name: const u8[:] }
struct Input { name: const u8[:] }

fn put(rows: Row[>..]&, x: Row) { rows.push(x); }

fn import_files(rows: Row[>..]&, files: const Input[:]) {
    for f in files { put(rows, Row { f.name }); }
}

fn main() {
    var rows: Row[>..] = [];
    var files: Input[>..] = [];
    files.push(Input { "a.csv" });
    import_files(rows, files);
    var other: i64[>..] = [];
    other.push(1);
    other.clear();
    print(rows.len);
}
```
```
error: cannot clear other while rows is still used: a reference into it was stored there at :9 (§5.1)
```

`other` is a list of integers that nothing points into. With `rows.push(Row { f.name })` in place of the call to `put`, the program is accepted.

### Code wrongly accepted, use after shrink

```goose
import std;

struct Row { v: i64[:] }
struct Input { v: i64[:] }

fn put(rows: Row[>..]&, x: Row) { rows.push(x); }

fn import_files(rows: Row[>..]&, files: const Input[:]) {
    for f in files { put(rows, Row { f.v }); }
}

fn second() {
    var keep: i64[>..] = [];
    keep.push(2);
    var rows: Row[>..] = [];
    var files: Input[>..] = [];
    files.push(Input { keep[..] });
    import_files(rows, files);
    print(rows.len);
}

fn first() {
    var n = 0;
    if n == 0 {
        var other: i64[>..] = [];
        other.push(1);
        var rows: Row[>..] = [];
        var files: Input[>..] = [];
        files.push(Input { other[..] });
        import_files(rows, files);
        other.clear();
        other.push(777);
        print(rows[0].v[0]);
    }
}

fn main() {
    second();
    first();
}
```

On master this compiles, and `goose run` prints `1` and then `777`: `rows[0].v` still views `other` after the clear. With `main` calling only `first`, it is rejected as it should be. So the verdict on `first` depends on whether another caller of `import_files` was checked before it.

### Cause

`ApplyCalleeStores` maps the events in the callee's record range onto the caller's arguments by rewriting `storeevents` in place:

1. The next call to the same specialization maps the events the previous call left behind, rooted at that caller's variables, which `mapped` keeps as they are. In the program above, `first`'s call to `import_files` sees the stores `second`'s call made.
2. A caller's event can name a holder local to the callee as its source. Here `rows` gets `src = x`, the parameter of `put`. Later calls rewrite that local's events, so what the caller's event means changes after the call. The same goes for a store into a lexical parent's variable from a nested function or a block (`fn put(r: Row) { rows.push(r); }` inside `main`, called through a generic).
3. The binding of a by-value holder parameter (`x` above) is recorded against the parameter's class, inexact. At the call it maps through `Bounds`, which drops the container the argument's views came out of (`from`, `classread`). So `x` holds "anything that outlives `files`", which includes `other` at the same depth. This is the false rejection. A holder parameter returned as it was passed (`fn pass(x: Row) -> Row { return x; }`) loses the same way in `RetAltVal`.

### Fix

1. `FnRecord::bodyevents` keeps, for each event in the range, where it pointed when the body's check ended. Each call maps from that, not from what the previous call left.
2. While a call maps the callee's events, it collects those of the callee's own holders (`calleelocals`). A copy of one of them, into the caller's storage through a class event (`push`) or into a variable through the range (a lexical parent's), is replaced by that holder's stores as mapped for this call, following copies between the callee's holders (computed once per strongly connected group of copies). A caller's events then never name a callee's local. Each copy is made once per container and each resulting store once, however often the callee repeats it. The copy event itself stays in the record for the next call; only its container is cleared, which the snapshot restores.
3. The binding event of a by-value holder parameter maps to the argument's contents as they are: an exact place as it is, a bound with the container it came from, as `NoteHolderBinding` records a local bound to the same value. Where the argument's contents are unknown it maps as before.
4. `RetRoot::heldparam` records that every return gives a by-value holder parameter as it was passed. `VarDef::storedinto` marks a variable any store other than a parameter's binding went into; `ResetLocal` keeps it across a cycle's rounds, since a later round may reuse a nested function's check from an earlier one, and `SameRecord` compares `heldparam`. The call's result is then the argument's contents, not the class's bound.

### Testing

New tests:
- `test/lifetimes/holder_param_stores.goose`: the first program, through `put`, through a helper that copies its parameter twice before pushing it, and through `pass`, with a second caller checked first. Rejected on master.
- `test/errors_tc/holder_param_stores_order.goose`: the second program. Compiles on master.
- `test/errors_tc/holder_param_stores_chain.goose`: a helper storing a copy of its own holder, reached through another function, with a second caller checked first. Compiles on master.
- `test/errors_tc/holder_param_stores_nested.goose`, `holder_param_stores_local.goose`: nested functions storing a holder parameter, or a copy of their own local holder, into the parent's variable. Rejected on master too; they guard the lexical stores of point 2.
- `test/errors_tc/holder_param_stores_recursive.goose`: a recursive function returning its holder parameter after a nested function stored into it. Rejected on master too; it guards point 4 across a cycle's rounds.
- `test/lifetimes/holder_param_stores_later.goose`: a holder argument read out of `rows` holding exact views, then `rows` gets a view of `a` after the call and `a` is cleared. Accepted on master too; it guards point 3's exact places.

