Following up on our chat: here is the use case, what goes wrong today, and what "attach/detach" would actually do. I'm opening this for discussion, not with a fixed design in mind.

### The use case

ent-lang (github.com/mzaks/ent-lang) runs a simulation's loops over entities on all cores. Some of the work inside those loops is done by "devices" in other languages, through plain C calls. The ones I'd like to write in Goose are pure functions, such as parsers, decoders and small numeric kernels:

- they read their arguments and return a value;
- they touch no mutable globals and do no I/O;
- they need scratch memory only for the duration of one call.

They don't communicate with anything. Each call is independent, but the calls come from the host's worker threads, many at the same time. If they all went through the main thread, the parallel loop would become a serial one, and the device would cost more than it saves.

### What happens today

The program starts no threads of its own, so the generated C has `GS_NEED_THREADS 0`. `GS_TLS` then expands to nothing, and `gs_stks` (the data stack block) is one plain global. Every exported wrapper calls into the function with `gs_sp = 0`, so all host threads bump the same `GS(0)->top` at once.

There is no crash, just wrong results. Repro (Goose 335fddc):

```goose
// n pushed n times and summed: always n * n
export "fill_sum" fn fill_sum(n: i32) -> i64 {
    var v: i64[>..] = [];
    for i in n { v.push(n); }
    var t: i64 = 0;
    for x in v { t += x; }
    return t;
}
```

```c
#include "fill.h"
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>
static long wrong[8];
static void *work(void *p) {
  long id = (long)p;
  for (int i = 0; i < 200000; i++) {
    int n = (i * 7 + id * 13) % 3000;
    if (fill_sum(n) != (long)n * n) wrong[id]++;
  }
  return 0;
}
int main(int argc, char **argv) {
  goose_init(argc, argv);
  pthread_t t[8];
  for (long i = 0; i < 8; i++) pthread_create(&t[i], 0, work, (void *)i);
  long all = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) { pthread_join(t[i], 0); all += wrong[i]; }
  printf("%ld of 1600000 wrong\n", all);
}
```

```
goose -o fill.c --header fill.h fill.goose
goose --emit-runtime rt.c && cc -O2 -c rt.c
cc -O2 host.c fill.c rt.o -o host -lm -pthread && ./host
→ 1309891 of 1600000 wrong
```

With `-DGS_NEED_THREADS=1` it segfaults instead, because the host's threads have no stack block (`gs_stks` is NULL on them).

### What "attach" means

Attaching means giving a host thread the same per-thread state a Goose worker gets when `gs_thread_spawn` starts it. That state is:

1. `gs_regions_begin(n)`: the thread's own region registry;
2. `gs_native_stack_init()`: its native stack bounds, for overflow checks;
3. `gs_stack_block(nstacks)`: its own block of data stacks;
4. `gs_gl`: a globals instance to point at.

"Detach" is the teardown a worker already does at its end: `gs_thread_release()` and `gs_release_regions()`.

Attaching starts no scheduler, sends no messages and shares no state. Each attached thread just gets private data stacks, so calls on different threads can't step on each other. A shim doing exactly those four steps per host thread made the repro exact on all 8 threads.

### Options, from smallest to largest

1. **Detect it and panic.** An exported wrapper checks that it runs on the thread that called `goose_init`, just as it already checks that `goose_init` was called. This keeps your "main thread only" model and turns silent corruption into a clear error. Even if nothing else changes, I think this one is worth having.
2. **Attach/detach for a host:** `goose_thread_attach()` and `goose_thread_detach()` in the `--header` output, doing the steps above. This also needs a way to get real TLS (`GS_NEED_THREADS 1`) when the program spawns no threads itself, for example whenever `--header` is used.
3. **Only exports that are safe to run in parallel.** Allow calls on attached threads only for exports that touch no mutable globals, which the compiler could check. This avoids the question of which globals instance an attached thread sees.
4. **Explicit context instead of TLS.** Exports take a `goose_ctx *` that the host creates once per thread. Nothing is hidden in thread-local state, but the code generator changes more.

Is any of these something you'd want in Goose? If you prefer to keep exports on the main thread only, option 1 alone would already help anyone who runs into this the way I did.

