## Bug

std lerp can return NaN or infinity at exact endpoints when b - a overflows, even though both endpoints are finite.

Verified on upstream master 3a0aa2e at JIT O0/O2 and native Clang O2.

## Minimal reproduction

    import std;
    fn main() {
        var x: f64[2] = [-1e308, 1e308];
        print(lerp(x[0], x[1], 0.0));
    }

Expected: -1e+308, the exact t == 0 endpoint.

Actual: nan. With t == 1 the same endpoints produce inf instead of 1e+308.

## Likely location and scope

stdlib/std.goose currently implements lerp as a + (b - a) * t. The intermediate difference overflows.

Endpoint branches or changing to (1-t)*a+t*b fix selected examples but do not by themselves establish robust full-domain behavior. A complete change should define and test opposite-sign extremes, finite interior interpolation, extrapolation, infinities, NaNs, signed zero, and both f32/f64 overloads.

## Bug

math inverse_lerp violates its documented endpoint result for large finite opposite-sign endpoints because its numerator and denominator overflow.

Verified on upstream master 3a0aa2e at JIT O0/O2 and native Clang O2.

## Minimal reproduction

    import math;
    fn main() {
        var x: f64[2] = [-1e308, 1e308];
        print(inverse_lerp(x[0], x[1], x[1]));
    }

Expected: 1.0. The stdlib documentation explicitly promises 0 at a and 1 at b.

Actual: nan. The midpoint also collapses to 0.0 instead of 0.5 for this range.

## Likely location and scope

stdlib/math.goose computes (v - a) / (b - a); both differences can overflow.

Special-casing v == a or v == b masks the endpoint reproduction but leaves extreme finite interior and extrapolated values broken. A complete solution likely needs scaling or another overflow-safe formulation, with reversed/equal endpoints, non-finite values, and both f32/f64 forms covered.

## Bug

math smoothstep can return NaN at a finite upper edge instead of its documented value 1 when normalization overflows.

Verified on upstream master 3a0aa2e at JIT O0/O2 and native Clang O2.

## Minimal reproduction

    import math;
    fn main() {
        var x: f64[2] = [-1e308, 1e308];
        print(smoothstep(x[0], x[1], x[1]));
    }

Expected: 1.0. The stdlib contract says smoothstep is 1 from edge1 onward.

Actual: nan.

## Likely location and scope

stdlib/math.goose computes (x - edge0) / (edge1 - edge0) before clamping. With these finite values that becomes inf / inf; NaN bypasses both clamp comparisons.

An early endpoint check fixes this exact upper-edge case but not extreme interior normalization, where overflow can also collapse the midpoint incorrectly. Any robust fix should coordinate with inverse_lerp behavior and preserve reversed-edge, equal-edge, NaN, and both f32/f64 semantics.

